<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  OWASP dependency-check suppression file for Muninn.

  Every entry below is a scoped suppression for a SPECIFIC CVE against a SPECIFIC
  package, added on the date noted in its <notes>, with the reasoning that makes it
  safe recorded inline. Do not add a blanket suppression (an empty packageUrl match,
  or a suppression with no <cve>/<cpe>) without discussing it first — see
  docs/steering/SECURITY_MODEL.md and the muninn-security-reviewer agent brief.

  Wired into the build via the org.owasp:dependency-check-maven plugin configuration
  in pom.xml (<suppressionFiles>), so both the local `mvn org.owasp:dependency-check-maven:check`
  invocation and the CI Security workflow (.github/workflows/security.yml) pick it up.
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">

    <!--
      2026-09-11 — CVE-2021-34538, CVE-2024-23953, CVE-2024-23945, CVE-2024-29869
      (all filed against the "Apache Hive" product).

      org.apache.orc:orc-core:1.9.8:nohive genuinely SHADES hive-storage-api 2.8.1
      (confirmed by unpacking orc-core-1.9.8-nohive.jar: it embeds a
      META-INF/maven/org.apache.hive/hive-storage-api/pom.properties fingerprint,
      which is what the scanner keys on). A version pin cannot reach code that's
      already compiled into someone else's jar, so the only real fixes are: (a)
      wait for org.apache.orc to re-shade against a patched hive-storage-api, or
      (b) confirm the vulnerable code isn't actually present in the shaded slice.

      We checked (b) directly. The classes orc-core actually shades in are narrowly
      org.apache.orc.storage.common.type.{HiveDecimal,FastHiveDecimal,HiveDecimalV1,
      HiveIntervalDayTime} and org.apache.orc.storage.serde2.io.HiveDecimalWritable* —
      i.e. just the decimal/interval type-conversion helpers ORC's own column-vector
      code needs. None of these four CVEs touch that code:
        - CVE-2021-34538 is a HiveServer2 semantic-analyzer authorization gap on
          CREATE/DROP FUNCTION (hive-exec).
        - CVE-2024-23953 is a non-constant-time signature comparison in the LLAP
          signer (hive-llap-common / hive-llap-server).
        - CVE-2024-23945 is a HiveServer2/Spark cookie-signer information exposure
          (hive-service).
        - CVE-2024-29869 is world-readable permissions on a Hive-written credentials
          temp file (hive-common / hive-exec).
      All four live in Hive modules that are never shaded into orc-core-nohive, and
      Muninn never runs HiveServer2, LLAP, or the Hive CLI in the first place — we
      use orc-core purely as ORC's read/write library via Iceberg
      (iceberg-data -> orc-core). Residual risk: if a future orc-core release
      changes what it shades, this suppression needs re-checking against the new
      jar contents; it is scoped to orc-core 1.9.x for that reason.

      CORRECTION 2026-09-11 (post-merge, verified against real dependency-check run
      34651381704): the FIRST version of this suppression keyed packageUrl against
      org.apache.orc/orc-core, which never matched — the "Unused Suppression Rule
      Analyzer" reported it as a zero-match rule, and all four CVEs still fired
      because dependency-check reports the finding against the *shaded* coordinate,
      pkg:maven/org.apache.hive/hive-storage-api@2.8.1, not against orc-core's own
      coordinate. Re-keyed below to the actual reported packageUrl. Lesson: verify a
      suppression matched (check the report / the "zero matches" log line) rather
      than assuming a plausible-looking regex is correct.
    -->
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
        2026-09-11: hive-storage-api CVEs shaded into orc-core-nohive do not affect the
        classes actually embedded (HiveDecimal/HiveDecimalWritable type helpers only);
        the vulnerable HiveServer2/LLAP/credential-file code lives in other Hive modules
        that are not present in this jar. See pom.xml dependencyManagement comment and
        PR description for the full writeup. Re-verify if orc-core's shaded contents change.
        Re-keyed 2026-09-11 to the packageUrl dependency-check actually reports
        (org.apache.hive:hive-storage-api, the shaded coordinate) after the original
        org.apache.orc:orc-core packageUrl was confirmed via the Unused Suppression Rule
        Analyzer to have zero matches.
        ]]></notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.apache\.hive/hive-storage-api@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2021-34538</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2024-23953</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2024-23945</cve>
        <cve>CVE-2024-29869</cve>
    </suppress>

    <!--
      2026-09-11 — CVE-2026-54285 ("OpenTelemetry Core Baggage DoS").

      This CVE is filed against the npm package @opentelemetry/core (opentelemetry-js):
      W3CBaggagePropagator.extract() failing to bound inbound baggage header size. It
      has no relationship to the Java io.opentelemetry:* artifacts — different
      ecosystem, different codebase, different maintainers. The scanner's CPE match (a
      shared "opentelemetry" vendor/product token) is a false positive. Confirmed via
      the GitHub advisory and NVD record, both of which describe the JS baggage
      propagator, not any class present in these Java jars.

      WIDENED 2026-09-11 (post-merge, verified against real dependency-check run
      34651381704): the original packageUrl only covered opentelemetry-api. The
      report shows the same misattributed finding independently against
      opentelemetry-sdk too (both are below the -DfailBuildOnCVSS=7 gate at 5.3, so
      neither was blocking the build, but the reasoning is identical for both and
      there's no reason to leave one unsuppressed). Widened to cover both by name
      rather than a blanket io.opentelemetry/.* match.
    -->
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
        2026-09-11: CVE-2026-54285 targets opentelemetry-js (@opentelemetry/core,
        W3CBaggagePropagator.extract()), not the Java io.opentelemetry:opentelemetry-api /
        opentelemetry-sdk artifacts. Product/ecosystem misattribution — no such
        baggage-propagation code exists in either jar. Widened from opentelemetry-api-only
        to also cover opentelemetry-sdk after the same misattributed finding turned up
        against it in dependency-check run 34651381704.
        ]]></notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/io\.opentelemetry/opentelemetry-(api|sdk)@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2026-54285</cve>
    </suppress>

    <!--
      2026-09-11 — CVE-2026-53914 ("JetBrains Kotlin Unsafe Deserialization").

      This is a vulnerability in the Kotlin COMPILER's build-cache metadata
      deserialization (fixed in Kotlin 2.4.20) — it affects IDEs/CI pipelines that
      invoke the Kotlin compiler and read back its build cache. Muninn is a pure-Java
      Maven build: no .kt sources, no kotlin-maven-plugin, the Kotlin compiler is never
      invoked. kotlin-stdlib / kotlin-stdlib-jdk7 / kotlin-stdlib-jdk8 are present only
      as runtime transitives of okhttp (via the zipkin OkHttp sender used by the
      Zipkin OTel exporter) — a plain runtime dependency on the standard library, not
      an invocation of the compiler or its build cache.
    -->
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
        2026-09-11: CVE-2026-53914 is a Kotlin COMPILER build-cache deserialization
        flaw (fixed 2.4.20). Muninn never invokes the Kotlin compiler (Java-only Maven
        build); kotlin-stdlib is present only as an okhttp/zipkin runtime transitive.
        Not reachable.
        ]]></notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.jetbrains\.kotlin/kotlin-stdlib.*@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2026-53914</cve>
    </suppress>

    <!--
      2026-09-11 — CVE-2020-29582 ("Incorrect Default Permissions in JetBrains Kotlin").

      Insecure temp-file/temp-dir creation API usage, fixed in Kotlin 1.4.21 (2020).
      Our resolved kotlin-stdlib-common is 1.9.10 — already five years of releases past
      the fix. Flagging it at all suggests the scanner's CPE range for this CVE has no
      upper bound for the "kotlin" product; suppressing rather than chasing a version
      bump that changes nothing (1.9.10 already contains the fix). Also not reachable
      in the first place: kotlin-stdlib-common is a transitive of okio-jvm (via the
      Zipkin OkHttp sender), and Muninn's own code never calls the affected temp-file
      helper.
    -->
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
        2026-09-11: CVE-2020-29582 fixed upstream in Kotlin 1.4.21; our resolved
        kotlin-stdlib-common (1.9.10) already exceeds that by several years of releases.
        Also not reachable — transitive of okio-jvm via the Zipkin OkHttp sender, and
        the affected temp-file API is never called from Muninn code.
        ]]></notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.jetbrains\.kotlin/kotlin-stdlib-common@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2020-29582</cve>
    </suppress>

    <!--
      2026-09-11 — CVE-2023-37475 (filed under "parquet-avro" in the 2026-09-11 scan
      output, but the CVE record itself is for github.com/hamba/avro — a Go-language
      Avro codec library unrelated to Apache Parquet or Apache Avro).

      The affected function (hamba/avro's Unmarshal, a Go API) and fix commit
      (b4a402f4, released in hamba/avro 2.13.0) belong to a different project entirely,
      in a different language ecosystem, with no code or dependency relationship to
      org.apache.parquet:parquet-avro. This is a product-name collision (both projects
      are informally "avro" tooling) rather than a real match. parquet-avro's own real
      Avro-schema CVE, CVE-2025-46762, is unrelated and already fixed on our resolved
      version (1.18.0, well past the 1.15.2 fix line) with no suppression needed.
    -->
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
        2026-09-11: CVE-2023-37475 is for github.com/hamba/avro (Go), not
        org.apache.parquet:parquet-avro (Java). Different project, different ecosystem,
        different vendor — a product-name collision, not a real match.
        ]]></notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.apache\.parquet/parquet-avro@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2023-37475</cve>
    </suppress>

    <!--
      2026-09-11 (post-merge follow-up, found via real dependency-check run
      34651381704) — CVE-2025-46762 reported against org.apache.iceberg:iceberg-parquet
      itself, in addition to (correctly) org.apache.parquet:parquet-avro.

      This one is a genuine scanner false positive, not a duplicate of a fixed finding.
      The dependency-check evidence for iceberg-parquet-1.11.0.jar shows it matched
      cpe:2.3:a:apache:parquet:1.11.0 with HIGHEST confidence — i.e. it took "parquet"
      out of iceberg-parquet's own package/artifact name (real evidence: vendor
      "apache"/"iceberg"/"parquet" strings all present in the jar) and combined it with
      iceberg-parquet's OWN version number (1.11.0) as if that were an Apache Parquet
      Java release. There has never been an Apache Parquet Java 1.11.0 — Iceberg and
      Parquet are versioned completely independently, and Iceberg's real transitive
      parquet-avro dependency (1.17.1 as declared in iceberg-parquet's own POM, 1.18.0
      as resolved in our build via the pin) is nowhere near 1.11.0. Confirmed via the
      canonical advisory (GHSA-53wx-pr6q-m3j5 / CVE-2025-46762): the only affected
      package listed is org.apache.parquet:parquet-avro (< 1.15.2); iceberg-parquet is
      not a separately-affected package. This is the same class of false positive as
      the Kotlin/opentelemetry entries above (vendor/product string collision), just
      discovered on a shaded/embedded-name basis rather than a shared-vendor basis.
    -->
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
        2026-09-11: CVE-2025-46762 against org.apache.iceberg:iceberg-parquet@1.11.0 is a
        false positive — dependency-check matched cpe:2.3:a:apache:parquet:1.11.0, but no
        Apache Parquet Java release 1.11.0 exists; Iceberg and Parquet version independently,
        and the CVE's own canonical advisory (GHSA-53wx-pr6q-m3j5) lists only
        org.apache.parquet:parquet-avro (< 1.15.2) as affected. Our real parquet-avro
        (1.18.0, pinned) is unaffected and correctly shows no finding for this CVE.
        ]]></notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.apache\.iceberg/iceberg-parquet@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2025-46762</cve>
    </suppress>

    <!--
      2026-09-11 (post-merge follow-up, found via real dependency-check run
      34651381704) — CVE-2026-73334, org.apache.parquet.crypto.keytools KMS-URL host
      validation gap (Parquet 1.12–1.18; a file-controlled KMS URL can be forwarded to a
      pluggable KmsClient without host validation, letting a malicious Parquet file
      redirect KMS token requests to an attacker-controlled host).

      UNLIKE the other suppressions in this file, this is NOT a false positive or an
      unreachable-forever code path: it is a real, currently-unpatched CVE in the exact
      parquet-avro/parquet-column version we ship (1.18.0). The upstream fix does not
      exist yet — the disclosure says "presumably" fixed in a future 1.19 release, and
      1.19.0 was not on Maven Central as of this check (latest available: 1.18.1,
      released 2026-09-04, before the 2026-09-08 disclosure). There is no version to
      pin to yet.

      The suppression is scoped to "not reachable today": Muninn does not use Parquet
      column encryption. Nothing in this codebase touches
      org.apache.parquet.crypto.keytools, configures a KmsClient, or sets a KMS URL
      (verified by grep across src/ for crypto/kms/encrypt — no matches). The vulnerable
      code path is present on the classpath but never exercised.

      RESIDUAL RISK — read before removing this suppression: if Muninn ever adds Parquet
      column-encryption/KMS support, this suppression MUST be revisited first, and the
      advisory's interim mitigation (application-controlled KMS URL, not file-controlled)
      applied. Track the upstream fix and bump to the first parquet release >= 1.19 that
      the Apache Parquet security advisory confirms as fixed, then remove this entry.
    -->
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
        2026-09-11: CVE-2026-73334 (org.apache.parquet.crypto.keytools KMS URL host
        validation, parquet 1.12-1.18) is a genuine, currently-unpatched CVE in our
        parquet-avro/parquet-column version (1.18.0) — no fixed release exists yet
        (1.19 not yet published as of this check; latest is 1.18.1). Suppressed as
        NOT REACHABLE TODAY: Muninn never uses Parquet column encryption / KMS client
        plugins (verified: no crypto/kms/encrypt usage anywhere in src/). This is a
        residual-risk suppression, not a false positive — revisit before adopting
        Parquet encryption, and replace with a real version pin once Parquet ships a
        confirmed fix (tracking: presumably >= 1.19).

        2026-09-11 (round 3): keyed to the whole org.apache.parquet group rather than
        an enumerated artifact list. The first attempt named only parquet-avro and
        parquet-column, and a real scan (run 34652326927) then failed on
        parquet-common, which carries the same CVE at the same version. Enumerating
        artifacts had already caused one miss in this same PR (the jetty websocket
        artifacts were missed when jetty-webapp/jetty-servlet were handled), so this
        entry is scoped by group + CVE. The CVE is in the shared keytools code and
        every parquet-* artifact ships at one version, so a narrower scope only
        risks another gap.
        ]]></notes>
        <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.apache\.parquet/parquet-.*@.*$</packageUrl>
        <cve>CVE-2026-73334</cve>
    </suppress>

</suppressions>
